"H4013":"fortification","H8384":"fig","H5973":"with","H1061":"firstfruit","H518":"if","H5128":"to shake","H5307":"fall","H6310":"lip","H398":"to eat","H2009":"behold","H5971":"people","H802":"woman","H7130":"among","H6605":"to open","H8179":"gate","H776":"country/planet","H784":"fire","H1280":"bar","H4692":"siege","H7579":"to draw","H2388":"strengthen","H935":"come","H2916":"mud","H7429":"to trample","H2563":"clay","H4404":"brick","H8033":"there","H3772":"cut","H3218":"locust","H697":"locust","H7235":"to multiply","H7402":"to trade","H3556":"star","H8064":"heaven","H6584":"to strip","H5774":"to fly","H4502":"prince","H2951":"official","H1462":"locust","H2583":"to camp","H1448":"wall","H3117":"day","H7135":"cold","H8121":"sun","H2224":"to rise","H3045":"to know","H4725":"place","H335":"where?","H5123":"to slumber","H7462":"House of Shepherds","H4428":"king","H804":"Assyria","H7931":"to dwell","H117":"great","H6335":"to leap","H2022":"mount","H6908":"to gather","H3545":"easing","H7667":"breaking","H2470":"weak","H4347":"wound","H8085":"hear","H8088":"sound","H8628":"to blow","H3709":"palm","H3588":"for","H5674":"to pass","H7451":"harmful","H8548":"continually"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["challoner",8,null,"challoner","Populous Alexandria.... No-Ammon. A populous city of Egypt destroyed by the Chaldeans, and afterwards rebuilt by Alexander, and called Alexandria. Others suppose No-Ammon to be the same as Diospolis."],["challoner",17,null,"challoner","Locusts of locusts.... The young locusts."],["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Blood. Nemrod established his power by shedding blood. Gen. x. Ninus, who built Ninive, and his successors were also bloody. After 1200 years the empire decayed under Sardanapalus, as historians agree. Yet it continued longer, according to the Scriptures and Ribera, till the Chaldees destroyed it, when it had subsisted about 1440 years. It was even possessed of great power after the return of the Jews from Babylon, as Eus. S. Aug. V. Bede, &c. write. (Worthington) — Depart. Septuagint \"be touched.\" (Haydock) — He continues the metaphor of the lion seizing its prey. Here the last chapter should end."],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","The noise. He has described the forces of Ninive, now he specifies those of Cyaxares and Nabopolassar."],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Harlot. Ninive is cruel and impure, engaging others in idolatry and witchcraft. (Calmet) — Sold, forcing them to adopt her manners. Rom. vii. 14."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Bemoan. Literally \"shake his head:\" the latter words are not in Hebrew (Haydock) — Some supply, move his lips: but head will answer as well. This is a sign of derision or of pity. Job xlii. 11. Mat. xxvii. 39. (Calmet)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Populous Alexandria. No-Amon. A populous city of Egypt, destroyed by the Chaldeans, and afterwards rebuilt by Alexander, and called Alexandria. Others suppose No-Amon to be the same as Diospolis. (Challoner) — This seems preferable, as it was amidst waters and near the Mediterranean. Profane historians take little notice of it, as it was greatly reduced. Bochart fixes upon Memphis, others upon the temple of Ammon. But these were too remote from the sea. (Calmet) — The former was however near the Nile, (Haydock) which is sometimes called a sea. (Calmet) — S. Jerom thinks that Alexandria stood on the ruins of No. (Worthington) — Yet of this we have no proof. It is thought that Nahum alludes to the devastation caused by Nabuchodonosor. As Juda however was still in his kingdom, it seems rather that Assaraddon, (Is. xx.) or his predecessor, Sennacherib, (Calmet) laid waste this city. 4 K. xviii. 21. Usher, A. 3292."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Ethiopia; Chus, in Arabia, not far from Diospolis."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Captivity. It was afterwards re-established and taken by Nabuchodonosor. (Calmet) — Fetters, or stocks. (Haydock)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Drunk, and be chastised by God. Ezec. xxiii. 32. — From, to escape."],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Water. This was a necessary precaution. 2 Par. xxxii. 3. — Brick, to repair the breaches."],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Locust. Yet all will be in vain. Thy numbers will be cut off as easily as locusts."],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Away. Thus did the merchants, at the approach of the enemy."],["haydock",17,null,"challoner","Guards. Hebrew \"crowned\" princes. — Little. Hebrew \"satraps are like great locusts, which,\" &c. S. Jerom has read (Calmet) toppic instead of taphseraic, (Haydock) which Septuagint neglect. Thapsar denotes an officer. Jer. li. 27. (Calmet) — Of locusts. The young locusts. (Challoner)"],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Slumbered. They have not guarded the flock. (Calmet)"],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Hidden. Hebrew and Septuagint \"irremediable.\" (Haydock) — No one pities thy wound. (Chaldean; Calmet)"]],"au":{"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"],"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]},"fa":[[1,null,0,"Homilies on John","Homily 64",0,407,"Let us mourn with Nahum, and say with him, \"Woe to him that buildeth his house on high. Or rather let us mourn for them as Christ mourned for those of old. \"Woe to you that are rich, for ye have received your consolation. Let us, I beseech you, not cease thus lamenting, and if it be not unseemly, let us even beat our breasts for the carelessness of our brethren. Let us not weep for him who is already dead, but let us weep for the rapacious man, the grasping, the covetous, the insatiable. Why should we mourn for the dead, in whose case it is impossible henceforth to effect anything? …"]],"fau":[["chrysostom","John Chrysostom",1,"father",null,1,407]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 14 (1889)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf114"]]}